    "section_evoo_v3_faq_rRJhLR": {
      "type": "section-evoo-v3-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "faq_item_BFc6Am": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How is Vitamin K2 different from Vitamin K1?",
            "answer": "<p>Vitamin K1 (phylloquinone) is found in leafy greens and plays a primary role in blood clotting its activity is largely confined to the liver. Vitamin K2    (menaquinone) travels beyond the liver to bones and arteries, where it activates    two critical proteins: Osteocalcin (for calcium binding in bone) and Matrix Gla    Protein or MGP (to prevent calcium from depositing in arteries). Most people get    adequate K1 from diet K2 is where the gap exists for bone and arterial health.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_item_pgG9dR": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why MK-7 and not MK-4?",
            "answer": "<p>Both are forms of Vitamin K2. MK-7 has a half-life of approximately 72 hours meaning a single daily dose maintains active K2 levels throughout the day and    into the next. MK-4 has a half-life of just 1–2 hours and requires multiple    higher doses per day to achieve comparable effect. For a daily supplement protocol,    MK-7 is the preferred clinical choice.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_item_zet6LP": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Who benefits most from AIWO Vitamin K2 MK-7?",
            "answer": "<p>Adults over 35 looking to support long-term bone density and arterial health,    individuals supplementing with Vitamin D3 (D3 increases calcium absorption K2 ensures that calcium is correctly directed), those with family history of osteoporosis or arterial calcification, and anyone looking to complete their calcium metabolism protocol. Your Certified Dietician will determine whether K2 MK-7 belongs in your protocol.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_item_Q6TUxh": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Should I take K2 with Vitamin D3?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes — Vitamin D3 and K2 MK-7 are synergistic. D3 significantly increases calcium absorption from the gut. K2 then activates the proteins that direct that calcium to bones (Osteocalcin) rather than arteries (MGP). Taking D3 without K2 increases circulating calcium — K2 ensures it ends up where it belongs. AIWO Vitamin K2 MK-7 pairs naturally with any D3 supplementation protocol.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_item_WyBt4K": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is AIWO Vitamin K2 safe if I am on blood thinners?",
            "answer": "<p>Vitamin K plays a role in coagulation. If you are on anticoagulant medication    such as warfarin or other Vitamin K antagonists, consult your doctor before   supplementing with any Vitamin K form. Do not self-supplement. Your Certified    Dietician will advise appropriately for your protocol.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "faq_item_BFc6Am",
        "faq_item_pgG9dR",
